{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_015Jd6Pq7d7mcDFCiGUNoxvV","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Let me analyze the project context carefully and propose the next task.\n\n## Project State Summary\n\n**Problem**: go-unix-utils is an experiment in specification-driven development using classic Unix utilities as the test bed. It derives formal specifications from C source code, generates Go implementations from those specs using an AI coding agent, and verifies parity against reference binaries through differential testing.\n\n**Architecture**: Four-stage pipeline (extract, specify, synthesize, verify) with:\n- `cmd/` - Go implementations of each utility\n- `pkg/sys/` - Darwin/Linux syscall abstractions\n- `pkg/format/` - Shared output formatting\n\n\n- `pkg/testutils/` - Differential testing harness\n- `magefiles/` - Build targets\n- `docs/` - Specifications\n\n**Current State**: The codebase already has implementations in place for the testing infrastructure, system abstractions, and output formatting. The `pkg/testutils/` package contains the differential testing harness, `pkg/sys/` provides platform-specific syscall wrappers for signals, file stats, and terminal operations, and `pkg/format/` handles shared output formatting with color support.\n\nThe command-line tools are mostly complete across the board—`cat`, `wc`, `sponge`, `du`, `version`, and `true` all have their main implementations and test files, though `true` is notably missing its test suite. Looking at the release timeline, there are eight versions in scope from 00.0 through 02.2, and I'm examining the current code status to understand what's been implemented so far.\n\nI'm noticing a significant discrepancy between what the status tracker says and what's actually in the source code. The earlier releases claim their code hasn't started, but the source files are already there—`wc`, `cat`, `sponge`, and `du` all have working implementations. For rel02.0, the specs are still pending for several utilities like `true`, `false`, `yes`, `basename`, and `dirname`, though `true` already has code written. The later releases (02.1 and 02.2) are still waiting on their specifications. So I'm seeing that cmd/true has implementation but no tests, while cmd/false, cmd/yes, basename, and dirname have no code at all yet. There are orphaned PRDs for true and false that exist but aren't properly linked. Looking at the release timeline, releases 00.0 and 01.0 appear complete based on the actual source code, so I need to focus on what's genuinely missing for the next incomplete release. Release 01.1: cat - has source code. Done.\nRelease 01.2: sponge - has source code. Done.\nRelease 01.3: du - has source code. Done.\n\nRelease 02.0: true, false, yes, basename, dirname\n- true: has main.go but missing tests, with an orphaned PRD (prd020-true)\n- false: orphaned PRD (prd021-false) exists but no implementation or tests\n- yes: no PRD, implementation, or tests\n- basename: no PRD, implementation, or tests\n- dirname: no PRD, implementation, or tests\n\nAll rel02.0 items show pending spec status with no PRs yet. Following the release-driven priority, I should tackle the earliest incomplete release first.\n\nSince true and false have PRDs already (even if orphaned), they're the most ready to move forward. The cmd/true implementation exists but lacks tests, so writing a test suite for it would be the highest-impact next step. the earliest incomplete release (02.0) would be:\n\nOption 1: Tests for cmd/true (since implementation exists but no tests)\nOption 2: Implementation of cmd/false (since PRD exists)\nOption 3: Tests for cmd/false\n\nSince cmd/true already has code and a PRD exists, writing differential tests for it is the logical next step. This is the earliest incomplete release, and true is the first use case listed.\n\nBut wait - do I need to check that the PRD for true actually exists? The analysis says \"orphaned PRD: prd020-true\" which means the file docs/specs/product-requirements/prd020-true.yaml exists. The cmd/true/main.go source code references \"prd020-true R1-R5\" in its header comment.\n\nSo the task should be: Write differential tests for cmd/true against the GNU reference binary. Since `true` is a coreutils utility, the reference would be `gtrue` from Homebrew's GNU coreutils. Looking at the existing test patterns, I'd need to set up a TestMain that builds the Go binary and locates the reference, then create DiffTest cases that compare outputs. The tricky part is that `true` doesn't produce stdout normally, so the tests would focus on exit codes and flag handling—checking that it exits with 0 silently with no args, exits with 0 when given arbitrary args, and properly handles --help and --version flags as the first argument only, while silently ignoring everything else. Writing differential test cases...\n\nI'm realizing that --help and --version output will differ between our implementation and GNU true, so I'd need to either normalize the output or test those separately. For the core behavior like exit codes and argument handling, differential testing works well, but the help and version flags might be better as unit tests since the text content will naturally vary between implementations.\n\nLet me scope this properly within the constraint of one task with at most four requirements, and write out the test task definition.\n\nWriting task definition... PRD for true utility)\n      - cmd/true/main.go (existing implementation to test)\n      - pkg/testutils/difftest.go (differential testing harness API)\n      - cmd/cat/cat_test.go (reference pattern for differential test structure)\n\n    files:\n      - path: cmd/true/true_test.go\n        action: create\n        note: Differential tests for cmd/true against GNU reference binary gtrue\n\n    requirements:\n      - id: R1\n        text: \"Test exit code 0 with no arguments and with arbitrary arguments per prd020-true R1\"\n      - id: R2\n        text: \"Test --help and --version as first argument per prd020-true R2 -- compare exit codes only since output text will differ between Go and GNU binaries\"\n      - id: R3\n        text: \"Test that non-first-argument --help/--version and all other arguments are silently ignored per prd020-true R1.2, R3\"\n      - id: R4\n        text: \"Follow the TestMain pattern from cmd/cat/cat_test.go: locate gtrue via exec.LookPath, build Go binary to temp dir, run differential tests via pkg/testutils.RunDiffTests\"\n\n    design_decisions:\n      - id: D1\n        text: \"Reference binary is gtrue (Homebrew coreutils g-prefix convention per ARCHITECTURE.yaml DD4)\"\n      - id: D2\n        text: \"Use pkg/testutils.RunDiffTests for all test cases that compare stdout, stderr, and exit code\"\n      - id: D3\n        text: \"For --help and --version tests, use a normalizer that clears stdout before comparison since the output text differs between implementations\"\n\n    acceptance_criteria:\n      - id: AC1\n        text: \"cmd/true/true_test.go exists and compiles\"\n      - id: AC2\n        text: \"go test ./cmd/true/ passes when gtrue is available on PATH\"\n      - id: AC3\n        text: \"Tests cover no-args, arbitrary args, --help, --version, and silently-ignored args\"\n      - id: AC4\n        text: \"TestMain follows the established pattern: LookPath for gtrue, build Go binary, cleanup on exit\"\n\nI'm realizing the key insight here: GNU true does print help text when given --help and exits with 0, just like our implementation. The actual text content will be different between the two versions, but the exit codes should match. So I need to set up the differential test to only compare exit codes for those flags, not the output text itself.\n\nLooking at how our implementation handles this, printHelp and printVersion both exit cleanly with code 0, while GNU true does the same. The simplest approach is to use RunDiffTests with a normalizer that strips stdout for the --help and --version cases specifically, letting us verify the exit codes match without worrying about the different help text content. help/--version, use a normalizer that strips all stdout (since we only care about exit code matching for those).\n\nHmm, but normalizers apply to ALL tests in a RunDiffTests call. So we'd need separate RunDiffTests calls with different normalizers.\n\nLooking at the test patterns again: each Test function calls RunDiffTests with its own set of tests and normalizers. So we can have:\n1. TestTrue_DefaultBehavior - no normalizer, tests no-args and arbitrary args (no stdout expected from either)\n2. TestTrue_HelpVersion - normalizer that clears stdout, tests --help and --version (compare exit codes only)\n3. TestTrue_IgnoredArgs - no normalizer, tests that non-first --help/--version are ignored\n\nThis is a reasonable structure. Let me finalize the task.\n\nActually, I realize I need to be more careful. Looking at what `gtrue` actually does: it exits 0 with no output for any args, but prints help or version to stdout when those flags come first. Our Go implementation matches this behavior. So for most cases the outputs should be identical between the two binaries, but for --help and --version we only need to compare exit codes since the help text itself might differ. I'm finalizing the task description to be self-contained for the stitch agent, making sure it clearly specifies using `gtrue` as the reference binary since `true` is a coreutils utility that follows the project's naming convention.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":87491,"cache_read_input_tokens":19289,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":87491},"output_tokens":10,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a92e8fa3-3139-43f8-a717-f5aad66310e0","uuid":"e8f8bf03-90d1-4aec-a16c-178524503641"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_015Jd6Pq7d7mcDFCiGUNoxvV","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"## Project State Summary\n\n**Problem**: go-unix-utils is a specification-driven development experiment that derives formal specs from C source code of Unix utilities, generates Go implementations via AI, and verifies parity through differential testing against GNU reference binaries.\n\n**Architecture**: Four-stage pipeline (extract → specify → synthesize → verify) with `cmd/` (one package per utility), `pkg/sys/` (syscall abstractions), `pkg/format/` (output formatting), `pkg/testutils/` (differential testing harness), and `magefiles/` (build targets).\n\n**Current state**: Source code exists for `pkg/testutils`, `pkg/sys`, `pkg/format`, `cmd/wc`, `cmd/cat`, `cmd/sponge`, `cmd/du`, `cmd/true`, and `cmd/version`. Releases 00.0 through 01.3 have implementations present in the source tree. Release 02.0 (trivial utilities: true, false, yes, basename, dirname) is the earliest incomplete release.\n\n**Current release**: rel02.0. `cmd/true` has an implementation (`main.go`) but no test file. PRDs exist for `true` (prd020-true, orphaned) and `false` (prd021-false, orphaned). The remaining utilities (yes, basename, dirname) have no PRDs or code yet.\n\n## Priority Reasoning\n\nThe earliest incomplete release is 02.0. Within it, `cmd/true` already has an implementation referencing prd020-true R1-R5 but lacks differential tests. Per dependency ordering (implementation before tests, and the implementation exists), the next task is writing tests for `cmd/true`. This is the highest-priority work: it validates existing code before moving to `cmd/false` or other utilities.\n\n```yaml\n- index: 0\n  title: \"cmd/true differential tests (prd020-true R1, R2, R3, R5)\"\n  dependency: -1\n  description: |\n    deliverable_type: code\n\n    required_reading:\n      - docs/specs/product-requirements/prd020-true.yaml (PRD for true utility -- requirements R1 through R5)\n      - cmd/true/main.go (existing implementation under test)\n      - pkg/testutils/difftest.go (DiffTest struct and RunDiffTests API)\n      - cmd/cat/cat_test.go (reference pattern for differential test file structure and TestMain)\n\n    files:\n      - path: cmd/true/true_test.go\n        action: create\n        note: Differential tests for cmd/true against GNU reference binary gtrue\n\n    requirements:\n      - id: R1\n        text: \"Test default behavior per prd020-true R1: no arguments exits 0 with no stdout and no stderr; arbitrary non-flag arguments exit 0 with no stdout and no stderr\"\n      - id: R2\n        text: \"Test --help and --version behavior per prd020-true R2: when --help is the first argument, exit code is 0; when --version is the first argument, exit code is 0. Use a normalizer that clears stdout before comparison since output text differs between Go and GNU binaries\"\n      - id: R3\n        text: \"Test argument ignoring per prd020-true R3: --help and --version as non-first arguments are silently ignored (exit 0, no output); short flags like -n and -v are silently ignored (exit 0, no output)\"\n      - id: R4\n        text: \"Follow the TestMain pattern from cmd/cat/cat_test.go: locate gtrue via exec.LookPath (skip if not found), build Go binary via go build to a temp directory, clean up temp directory after tests complete\"\n\n    design_decisions:\n      - id: D1\n        text: \"Reference binary is gtrue (Homebrew coreutils g-prefix convention per ARCHITECTURE.yaml DD4). If gtrue is not found on PATH, print a skip message and exit 0 from TestMain, matching the pattern in cmd/cat/cat_test.go\"\n      - id: D2\n        text: \"Use pkg/testutils.RunDiffTests for all test cases. Group tests into separate Test functions by requirement area: TestTrue_DefaultBehavior (R1), TestTrue_HelpVersion (R2), TestTrue_IgnoredArgs (R3)\"\n      - id: D3\n        text: \"For --help and --version tests, pass a NormalizeFunc that replaces all stdout bytes with an empty slice before comparison, since help/version text content differs between Go and GNU implementations but exit code and stderr must match\"\n\n    acceptance_criteria:\n      - id: AC1\n        text: \"cmd/true/true_test.go exists and compiles with go build ./cmd/true/\"\n      - id: AC2\n        text: \"go test ./cmd/true/ passes when gtrue is available on PATH\"\n      - id: AC3\n        text: \"Tests cover: no-args exit 0, multiple arbitrary args exit 0, --help as first arg exits 0, --version as first arg exits 0, --help as non-first arg is ignored, short flags are ignored\"\n      - id: AC4\n        text: \"TestMain follows the established pattern: exec.LookPath for gtrue, go build to temp dir, m.Run, os.RemoveAll cleanup\"\n```"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":87491,"cache_read_input_tokens":19289,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":87491},"output_tokens":10,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a92e8fa3-3139-43f8-a717-f5aad66310e0","uuid":"88154290-cda7-4573-b830-aec6d38da6f2"}
